# Line endings.
#
# This matters more than it looks. Cloudflare builds on Linux and runs
# `sh build.sh`. If build.sh is committed with CRLF, the shell reads the trailing
# \r as part of the last token on every line and the build dies on something like
# "build.sh: line 2: $'\r': command not found" — a failure that is invisible on
# Windows, where everything runs fine.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on this machine, so without this file git would have
# happily stored CRLF. eol=lf pins the shell scripts in BOTH the repo and the
# working copy; text=auto normalises everything else on the way in.

* text=auto

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.svg  text eol=lf
*.pdb  text eol=lf
_headers text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Batch files are read by cmd.exe on Windows and stay CRLF.
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Never touched.
*.png  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.pptx binary

# Raw uploads waiting to be converted. Mangling one is silent corruption:
# git would rewrite bytes inside a JPEG that happen to look like line endings.
*.heic binary
*.heif binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.tif  binary
*.tiff binary
